[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Nullable Types in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, value types (like int, bool, double, struct, etc.) cannot be directly assigned a null value because they are designed to always hold a value. However, there are situations where you need a value type to represent a missing or undefined value. This is where nullable types come in."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What are Nullable Types?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A nullable type allows you to assign a null value to value types. It's essentially a wrapper around the underlying value type that indicates whether the value is present or not. Nullable types are defined using the '?' operator after the value type."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"int? nullableInt = null; // nullableInt can hold an integer value or null
bool? nullableBool = true; // nullableBool can hold a boolean value or null
double? nullableDouble = 3.14; // nullableDouble can hold a double value or null"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaring Nullable Types"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can declare a nullable type using the following syntax:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"ValueType? variableName;"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"int? age; // 'age' can be an integer or null
bool? isEmployed; // 'isEmployed' can be true, false, or null"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Properties of Nullable Types"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HasValue: A boolean property that indicates whether the nullable type has a value assigned to it. Returns 'true' if the nullable type has a value, and 'false' if it's null."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Value: Returns the value of the nullable type if 'HasValue' is 'true'. If 'HasValue' is 'false', accessing 'Value' will throw an 'InvalidOperationException'. Always check 'HasValue' before accessing 'Value'."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"int? quantity = null;

if (quantity.HasValue)
{
    int actualQuantity = quantity.Value;
    Console.WriteLine("Quantity: " + actualQuantity);
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Quantity is not specified.");
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"The Null-Coalescing Operator (?)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The null-coalescing operator ('??') provides a concise way to assign a default value to a variable if the nullable type is null. It returns the value of the left-hand operand if it's not null; otherwise, it returns the value of the right-hand operand."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"int? nullableValue = null;
int result = nullableValue ?? 0; // If nullableValue is null, result will be 0

Console.WriteLine("Result: " + result); // Output: Result: 0

nullableValue = 10;
result = nullableValue ?? 0; // nullableValue is not null, so result will be 10

Console.WriteLine("Result: " + result); // Output: Result: 10"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Nullable Types and Boxing/Unboxing"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a nullable type is boxed, the boxed object contains the value of the underlying type if 'HasValue' is 'true'. If 'HasValue' is 'false' (i.e., the nullable type is null), the boxed object is a null reference."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"int? nullableInt = 5;
object boxedInt = nullableInt; // Boxing

if (boxedInt != null)
{
    int unboxedInt = (int)boxedInt; // Unboxing
    Console.WriteLine("Unboxed value: " + unboxedInt);
}

nullableInt = null;
boxedInt = nullableInt; // Boxing a null nullable

if (boxedInt == null)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Boxed value is null.");
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases for Nullable Types"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Database Interactions: Representing optional fields in database tables where a column might allow null values."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configuration Settings: Handling optional configuration settings where a value might not be provided."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parsing: Representing the result of a parsing operation that might fail (e.g., parsing a string to an integer)."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nullable types are a powerful feature in C# that allows you to represent missing or undefined values for value types. They are essential for handling scenarios where a value might not always be available, such as database interactions, configuration settings, and parsing operations. Understanding how to use nullable types effectively can lead to more robust and maintainable code."}]
